// sys_liveness.strata -- SYS200-204 contention-family liveness litmus
// (T-4993, SF-01's "79 rule ids, zero telemetry fires" measurement).
//
// A minimal, real design that plants the SYS204 unarbitrated resource-
// mode-conflict violation end to end through the real surface grammar
// (parse_module -> elaborate -> resource_contention_violations), used by
// tests/gates_suite/test_sys_rule_liveness.py as the one design-level
// positive control alongside the per-rule Python-model fixtures the rest
// of that suite's registry-to-fixture mapping points at. Two nodes access
// the SAME named resource in conflicting modes (`write`/`write`) with no
// `resource { arbitrated_by ... }`/`lock` declared anywhere in this
// module, so `resource_contention_violations` must report SYS204 for the
// pair -- exactly the T-0700 acceptance criterion
// `tests/unit/strata/test_access.py::TestResourceContentionViolations::
// test_two_writers_no_arbiter_fires` already proves at the KernelModel
// level; this file proves the SAME obligation survives real surface
// syntax (parse -> elaborate), not just a hand-built model.
//
// tested end to end (parse -> elaborate -> resource_contention_violations)
// by tests/gates_suite/test_sys_rule_liveness.py; a permanent CI golden
// for this ticket's registry-driven liveness sweep.

module sys_liveness

node ledger_writer_a : trusted {
    clearance Internal;
    access "ledger_db" mode write;
}

node ledger_writer_b : trusted {
    clearance Internal;
    access "ledger_db" mode write;
}
